Three things a Compose target got wrong about a method and a weight, each of
which reached Kotlin as a name nothing declared.

A method on a struct or an enum is emitted as an extension function, so it has
to be *called* as one. The call site asked the codegen scope first, and the
package scope answers for every func it holds -- a method included -- so
`func Item.label()` resolved to its bare name and came out as `label(it)`
against a declaration spelled `fun Item.label()`. Declaration and call now ask
one function, `kotlin.ReceiverIsUserType`.

A native struct *is* the host type, so `IRTypeToKt` answers with the name the
mark carries rather than the declaration's own -- the rule `IRTypeToGo` already
applied. Without it android's own `StrokeCap` reached every signature it was
written in as an undeclared bare name.

And `weight()` takes a Float where a SNGL float is a Double, which the `f`
suffix settles for a literal and only a literal: appended to an expression it
made `entry.span` into `entry.spanf`.

deny out/kotlin/android/MainScreen.kt `label\(it\)` -- a method is called on its receiver, not handed it
deny out/kotlin/android/MainScreen.kt `spanf` -- the float suffix is a literal's; an expression converts

-- m.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

struct Item {
    n int = 0
    span float = 1

    func label() string {
        if this.n > 2 {
            return "big"
        }
        return "n=" + string(this.n)
    }
}

window #w {
    var it = Item{n=3}

    hbox {
        text(value=it.label(), style={flex=it.span})
    }
    button(text="bump", @click { it = Item{n=it.n + 1} })
}
